+
+Clock-Skew Leeway
+-----------------
+
+In distributed systems, clocks on different hosts are rarely perfectly
+in sync. A token signed on a host whose clock is a few seconds ahead of
+the verifying host will appear to come from the future and be rejected.
+Conversely, the token may expire a few seconds early on a verifier with
+a lagging clock.
+
+The ``leeway`` parameter on :meth:`~TimestampSigner.unsign`,
+:meth:`~TimestampSigner.validate`, :meth:`~TimedSerializer.loads`, and
+:meth:`~TimedSerializer.loads_unsafe` adds a small tolerance (in
+seconds) to both sides of the valid time window.  It must be a
+non-negative :class:`int` and requires ``max_age`` to be set.
+
+.. code-block:: python
+
+    from itsdangerous import TimestampSigner
+    s = TimestampSigner('secret-key')
+    token = s.sign('hello')
+
+    # Accept tokens up to 5 seconds outside the window to tolerate clock drift.
+    s.unsign(token, max_age=300, leeway=5)
+
+.. warning::
+
+    Leeway widens the acceptance window in **both** directions (too old
+    and slightly in the future).  Keep it small — typically 1–10 seconds.
+    Large values significantly weaken the expiry guarantee.
+
+
